## formula
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change = Heat for a temperature change
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change-q-name = heat
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change-meaning-school = energy the body received (Q > 0) or gave away (Q < 0)
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change-c-name = specific heat capacity
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change-meaning-school-2 = joules needed to warm 1 kg of this substance by 1 °C
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change-m-name = mass
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change-t-name = temperature change
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change-meaning-school-3 = how many degrees the body warmed (or cooled, if negative)
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change-explanation-novice = The more water and the more you want to heat it, the more energy it takes. Water is «demanding»: it needs about 10 times more energy than the same mass of copper.
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change-explanation-school = To heat a body you have to give it energy. The larger the mass and the bigger the warming, the more energy is needed. Water needs about 4200 J per kg per °C, copper only about 400 J.
formula-thermal-heat-for-temperature-change-explanation-expert = Relation between internal energy and temperature at constant c; in the model — the ΔU → ΔT conversion at every step.
formula-thermal-latent-heat = Latent heat of melting / vaporisation
formula-thermal-latent-heat-m-name = mass that melted (froze) or boiled away
formula-thermal-latent-heat-variables-name = specific latent heat of fusion or vaporisation
formula-thermal-latent-heat-meaning-school = joules needed to change the phase of 1 kg without changing its temperature
formula-thermal-latent-heat-explanation-novice = Melting ice or boiling water away takes a lot of energy, even though the thermometer shows the same number.
formula-thermal-latent-heat-explanation-school = Melting 1 kg of ice takes ≈ 334 kJ — as much as warming 1 kg of water by almost 80 °C. Boiling away 1 kg of water takes ≈ 2256 kJ, several times more.
formula-thermal-evaporation-rate = Evaporation rate
formula-thermal-evaporation-rate-variables-name = mass evaporating per second
formula-thermal-evaporation-rate-h-m-name = mass transfer coefficient
formula-thermal-evaporation-rate-a-name = open liquid surface area
formula-thermal-evaporation-rate-s-name = saturated vapour density above the liquid
formula-thermal-evaporation-rate-variables-name-2 = vapour density in the air (or under the lid)
formula-thermal-evaporation-rate-thermal-formulas-beyond-curriculum = Evaporation is taught qualitatively; the formula is beyond the curriculum.
formula-thermal-evaporation-rate-explanation-novice = Hot water steams — and a lot of energy leaves with the steam.
formula-thermal-evaporation-rate-explanation-school = The hotter the liquid and the larger its open surface, the more vapour leaves every second. Each kilogram of vapour carries ≈ 2.3 MJ — which is why a lid speeds up boiling so much.
formula-thermal-heat-capacity = Heat capacity of a body
formula-thermal-heat-capacity-c-name = heat capacity of the body
formula-thermal-heat-capacity-explanation-novice = A bucket of water is harder to heat than a glass, though the substance is the same.
formula-thermal-heat-capacity-explanation-school = How many joules it takes to warm this particular body by 1 °C. A bucket of water is «harder» to heat than a glass, though the substance is the same.
formula-thermal-equilibrium-temperature = Temperature of a mixture (heat balance)
formula-thermal-equilibrium-temperature-variables-name = mixture temperature
formula-thermal-equilibrium-temperature-bodies-name = bodies being mixed (mass, heat capacity, temperature)
formula-thermal-equilibrium-temperature-explanation-novice = Mix hot and cold water and you get warm water. The more hot water there was, the warmer the result.
formula-thermal-equilibrium-temperature-explanation-school = Hot bodies give away exactly the heat the cold ones receive. So the final temperature is an «average» in which each body counts as much as its heat capacity.
formula-thermal-conduction-conductance = Conductance of a rod or wall
formula-thermal-conduction-conductance-g-name = thermal conductance
formula-thermal-conduction-conductance-meaning-school = watts of heat passing per degree of temperature difference
formula-thermal-conduction-conductance-k-name = thermal conductivity of the material
formula-thermal-conduction-conductance-a-name = cross-section area
formula-thermal-conduction-conductance-l-name = length (thickness)
formula-thermal-conduction-conductance-thermal-formulas-beyond-curriculum = Schools treat conduction qualitatively; the formula is beyond the basic course.
formula-thermal-conduction-conductance-explanation-novice = A metal spoon in hot tea heats up quickly, a wooden one does not: metal conducts heat well.
formula-thermal-conduction-conductance-explanation-school = Heat passes more easily through a thick, short rod than a thin, long one. Copper conducts heat hundreds of times better than glass.
formula-thermal-convection-conductance = Convective conductance
formula-thermal-convection-conductance-meaning-school = watts of heat leaving per degree of temperature difference
formula-thermal-convection-conductance-h-name = heat transfer coefficient
formula-thermal-convection-conductance-meaning-school-2 = how well air or water «takes» heat from each square metre; larger in the wind
formula-thermal-convection-conductance-a-name = surface area
formula-thermal-convection-conductance-thermal-formulas-beyond-curriculum = Schools treat convection qualitatively; the formula is beyond the basic course.
formula-thermal-convection-conductance-explanation-novice = The larger the surface, the faster a body cools. We cool faster in the wind because moving air carries heat away better.
formula-thermal-convection-conductance-explanation-school = The larger the surface, the faster a body gives heat to the air. Moving air (wind) takes heat better than still air.
formula-thermal-series-conductance = Heat passing layers in series
formula-thermal-series-conductance-g-name = total conductance
formula-thermal-series-conductance-g-name-2 = conductance of the vessel wall
formula-thermal-series-conductance-g-name-3 = conductance of the transfer to water
formula-thermal-series-conductance-thermal-formulas-beyond-curriculum = Beyond the curriculum; a direct analogy of resistors in series.
formula-thermal-series-conductance-explanation-novice = Heat must pass two «obstacles» one after another: the vessel wall and the water layer next to it. The hardest obstacle slows heating the most.
formula-thermal-series-conductance-explanation-school = Like resistors in series: thermal resistances add up. A thin metal wall adds almost nothing, a glass wall adds noticeably.
formula-thermal-heat-flow = Heat flow between bodies
formula-thermal-heat-flow-p-name = heat flow rate
formula-thermal-heat-flow-meaning-school = joules of heat passing every second
formula-thermal-heat-flow-g-name = conductance of the link
formula-thermal-heat-flow-t-name = temperature of the first body
formula-thermal-heat-flow-t-name-2 = temperature of the second body
formula-thermal-heat-flow-thermal-formulas-beyond-curriculum = The idea «from hot to cold» is taught; the quantitative formula is beyond the basic course.
formula-thermal-heat-flow-explanation-novice = Heat flows from hot to cold — like water flows downhill. The bigger the «drop», the stronger the flow.
formula-thermal-heat-flow-explanation-school = Heat flows from hot to cold, and faster for a bigger temperature difference. If P < 0, heat flows the other way.
formula-thermal-energy-from-power = Energy from a heater
formula-thermal-energy-from-power-e-name = energy delivered
formula-thermal-energy-from-power-p-name = heater power
formula-thermal-energy-from-power-t-name = operating time
formula-thermal-energy-from-power-explanation-novice = While the heater is on, every second it adds a fixed portion of energy. The size of that portion is the power.
formula-thermal-energy-from-power-explanation-school = A 2000 W heater releases 2000 J of heat every second.
formula-thermal-energy-balance = Energy balance of a body
formula-thermal-energy-balance-u-name = change of internal energy
formula-thermal-energy-balance-q-in-name = heat received
formula-thermal-energy-balance-q-out-name = heat given away
formula-thermal-energy-balance-explanation-novice = Heat that came in, minus heat that went out, is what stayed in the body.
formula-thermal-energy-balance-explanation-school = Like a bank account: money in minus money out is how much the balance changed.
formula-thermal-time-constant = Cooling time constant
formula-thermal-time-constant-variables-name = time constant
formula-thermal-time-constant-thermal-formulas-beyond-curriculum = Beyond the school curriculum.
formula-thermal-time-constant-explanation-school = In time τ the difference from room temperature shrinks about 2.7 times. A large body with poor heat transfer (a thermos) cools slowly.
formula-thermal-newton-cooling = Newton's cooling curve
formula-thermal-newton-cooling-tt-name = temperature at time t
formula-thermal-newton-cooling-t-name = initial temperature
formula-thermal-newton-cooling-t-env-name = temperature of the surroundings
formula-thermal-newton-cooling-t-name-2 = time
formula-thermal-newton-cooling-thermal-formulas-beyond-curriculum = Beyond the school curriculum; shown as the theory curve next to the simulation.
formula-thermal-newton-cooling-explanation-school = Hot tea cools quickly at first, then ever more slowly, smoothly approaching room temperature.
formula-thermal-mass-fraction = Mass fraction of a dissolved substance
formula-thermal-mass-fraction-w-name = mass fraction
formula-thermal-mass-fraction-m-name = mass of the dissolved substance
formula-thermal-mass-fraction-m-name-2 = mass of the solution
formula-thermal-mass-fraction-meaning-school = the water and the dissolved substance together
formula-thermal-mass-fraction-explanation-novice = Divide the salt’s mass by the whole solution’s — and you know what part it makes.
formula-thermal-mass-fraction-explanation-school = w = m_solute / m_solution; the whole solution, water and substance together, goes below.
formula-thermal-crystals-on-cooling = How much comes out as crystals
formula-thermal-crystals-on-cooling-m-name = mass of the crystals
formula-thermal-crystals-on-cooling-s-name = solubility when hot
formula-thermal-crystals-on-cooling-s-name-2 = solubility when cold
formula-thermal-crystals-on-cooling-m-name-2 = mass of the water
formula-thermal-crystals-on-cooling-explanation-novice = Cold water holds less of a substance than hot — the rest comes out as crystals.
formula-thermal-crystals-on-cooling-explanation-school = Solubility S is grams of a substance per 100 g of water. Cooling, a saturated solution gives off the difference (S₁ − S₂) as crystals for every 100 g of water.
formula-thermal-heat-of-solution = Heat of solution
formula-thermal-heat-of-solution-q-name = heat of solution
formula-thermal-heat-of-solution-n-name = amount of substance
formula-thermal-heat-of-solution-meaning-school = the mass over the molar mass: n = m / M
formula-thermal-heat-of-solution-h-name = molar heat of solution
formula-thermal-heat-of-solution-explanation-novice = The more substance dissolved, the more the temperature changed.
formula-thermal-heat-of-solution-explanation-school = Q = n·ΔH: ΔH > 0 — heat is taken in (the solution cools), ΔH < 0 — given off (it warms).
formula-thermal-gas-density = Density of a gas
formula-thermal-gas-density-variables-name = density of the gas
formula-thermal-gas-density-p-name = pressure
formula-thermal-gas-density-m-name = molar mass
formula-thermal-gas-density-t-name = absolute temperature
formula-thermal-gas-density-r-name = gas constant
formula-thermal-gas-density-thermal-formulas-beyond-curriculum = The ideal gas law is beyond the grades 7–9 programme; in grade 7 — qualitatively: warm gas is lighter than cold, helium lighter than air.
formula-thermal-gas-density-explanation-novice = The lighter a gas’s molecules and the warmer it is, the lighter the gas.
formula-thermal-gas-density-explanation-school = At the same pressure and temperature a gas’s density is proportional to its molar mass: helium (4 g/mol) is about seven times lighter than air (29 g/mol). Heated, a gas expands and thins: its density goes inversely as the absolute temperature.
